# Gem versioning

When doing work that changes this gem, bump the version appropriately. Typically bump it only once per branch.

## Check the remote version before committing

Before selecting the version or committing code to the working branch:

- Fetch the latest remote default branch.
- Compare the working branch’s gem version with the version on the latest remote default branch.
- Ensure the working branch’s version is strictly greater than the latest remote default-branch version.
- Never lower or reuse a version already present on the default branch.

## Avoid repeated version bumps

Do not bump the version again for subsequent changes on the same branch unless:

- The earlier version bump has already been merged and a new release is required.
- Another change has raised the default branch’s version to the same or a higher version.

In the second case, bump the working branch’s version again so it is strictly greater than the latest remote default-branch version.

## Recheck before committing

Repeat the remote version check:

- After rebasing or merging the default branch into the working branch.
- Immediately before committing code to the working branch.

If the remote default-branch version has become equal to or greater than the working branch’s version, bump the working branch’s version again before committing.

## Release and upgrade notes

After doing work and pushing to a PR, provide or update release notes on what has changed.

If this is a gem others will use, provide upgrade instructions. If changes affect implementation, configuration, public APIs, dependencies, or expected behaviour, add concise upgrade notes explaining any required actions for existing users.
